{"author":{"id":"a3a282966ced55effb4560271fcd98e13241af24ea76b00ec76a0f18154bc8ee"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790098342,"nonce":"t8tgUK4vbu3PTC0a/IFjy2dNxjc=","title":"Decision: own the issue model and migrate the store once; config is many entities; four built-in fields","message":"Decided 2026-09-22 with Luis, in review of the config entity design. Three calls, recorded in doc/design/configurable-schema.md (D2, D3, D5, 'What changed') and doc/design/config-entity.md (E1, E4, 'Why not one entity').\n\n1. Own the issue model; migrate the store once. entities/bug was never in the pristine seven, but the design had been protecting its on-disk format: every old op kept readable forever, SetStatusOp reinterpreted, Snapshot.Status kept as a projection. That carries git-bug's model (title/status/labels as special struct members) for the life of the store, to protect callers that every other story is rewriting or removing anyway. Instead: package entities/issue, a structural core (id, author, timestamps, comments, timeline, participants, relations, fields map) plus a fields map; SetTitleOp/SetStatusOp/LabelChangeOp cease to exist; formatVersion bumps to 5 so the dag's version gate refuses old binaries rather than letting them misread. A one-time script rewrites refs/issues/* preserving entity ids (create op bytes unchanged) and comment ids (op ids hash the op, not the pack). One team, no external clones, and misc/migrate did this once already. entity/dag and the rest of the seven stay pristine.\n\n2. Config is one entity per configurable thing (field, type, relation, flow, view, rule) under refs/config/*, not one entity with a flat map of paths. The dag already gives one CRDT per entity and the entity boundary is the merge unit the one-entity draft was simulating; the draft also needed a singleton with a duplicate race no op could repair. Many entities need neither, and removal is an archive op that reaches every clone. Six generic ops: Create{kind,key}, SetAttr, RemoveAttr, SetItem, RemoveItem, SetArchived.\n\n3. First-class = the structural core, plus four built-in fields present in every schema and unremovable: title, type, status (enum with category), archived (bool). Status and archived stay separate (4d61ebe). Everything else — assignee, priority, estimate, dates, iteration, labels — is preset config found by kind and role, never by key. Iterations are the same entity as issues in refs/iterations, with fields declared on: iteration; capacity is a number field, not first class.\n\nReversed by this: the story doc's finding 1 conclusion (do not bump formatVersion), D3's compatibility shim, and AGENTS.md's 'Go package names stay bug so upstream fixes cherry-pick'.","files":null}]}